{"title":"IAT (IISER)","category":"Engineering","bank":"iat_iiser","subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","count":11,"questions":[{"id":23816,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Provided below are recognition sequences for restriction enzymes 1 (RE1), 2 (RE2) and 3 (RE3). Arrows indicate the positions where the enzymes digest on the two strands. Which of the following can the RE1 digested DNA ligate to?<br/><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fiat%252F1vHUMBjzLb1TtLA6nNZ2kMsSHe5lXtN92x43YU0FJyg.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2F1vHUMBjzLb1TtLA6nNZ2kMsSHe5lXtN92x43YU0FJyg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fiat%2F1vHUMBjzLb1TtLA6nNZ2kMsSHe5lXtN92x43YU0FJyg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>","options":["All three RE1, RE2 and RE3 digested DNA.","Only to RE1 digested DNA.","Only to RE1 and RE3 digested DNA.","Only to RE1 and RE2 digested DNA."],"answer":0,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2017","_marksId":"67fdff3d378e1624ea7b692a"},{"id":23817,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Which among the following correctly represents the sequence of events for a normal polymerase chain reaction?","options":["Denaturation, annealing, extension","Annealing, denaturation, extension","Extension, annealing, denaturation","Denaturation, extension, annealing"],"answer":0,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Easy","source":"IAT (IISER) 2019","_marksId":"67fdff52378e1624ea7b6a27"},{"id":23818,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"The pBR322 cloning vector has genes coding for tetracycline and ampicillin resistance. A foreign DNA to be cloned is inserted into the tetracycline resistance gene and the recombinant plasmid is then transformed into <em>E. coli</em> cells. Which one of the following choices is the most likely outcome of this cloning reaction?","options":["The cells with the recombinant plasmid can grow in the presence of ampicillin but not tetracycline","The cells with the recombinant plasmid can grow in the presence of both ampicillin and tetracycline","The cells with the non-recombinant plasmid can grow in the presence of ampicillin but not tetracycline","The cells with the non-recombinant plasmid can grow in the presence of tetracycline but not ampicillin"],"answer":0,"solution":"The pBR322 vector contains two antibiotic resistance genes: ampicillin resistance ($amp^{R}$) and tetracycline resistance ($tet^{R}$).<br><br>When a foreign DNA is inserted into the tetracycline resistance gene, the gene is disrupted. This phenomenon is known as insertional inactivation.<br><br>As a result, the recombinant plasmid loses its ability to confer resistance to tetracycline, while the ampicillin resistance gene remains intact and functional.<br><br>Therefore, E. coli cells transformed with the recombinant plasmid will be able to grow on a medium containing ampicillin but will fail to grow on a medium containing tetracycline.<br><br>Cells with the non-recombinant plasmid will retain both resistance genes and can grow in the presence of both antibiotics.<br><br>Answer: The cells with the recombinant plasmid can grow in the presence of ampicillin but not tetracycline","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2026","_marksId":"6a4521818c20d3b91d64f952"},{"id":23819,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"As opposed to DNA replication within the cell, discontinuous synthesis of DNA does NOT occur in a polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Why?","options":["Denaturation step in PCR substitutes for the replication fork and Taq polymerase extends DNA only in the $3^{\\prime}$ to $5^{\\prime}$ direction.","The replication fork is formed and DNA ligase activity of the Taq polymerase joins the discontinuous fragments.","The replication fork is formed and Taq polymerase extends DNA in both $3^{\\prime}$ to $5^{\\prime}$ and $5^{\\prime}$ to $3^{\\prime}$ direction.","Denaturation step in PCR substitutes for the replication fork and Taq polymerase extends DNA only in the $5^{\\prime}$ to $3^{\\prime}$ direction."],"answer":3,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2022","_marksId":"67fdff67378e1624ea7b6b0e"},{"id":23820,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is used to amplify a gene of interest (GOI). If, after 30 cycles of PCR, 1 billion copies of GOI are produced, approximately how many copies of GOI were present at the end of the $20^{t h}$ cycle?","options":["1 million","0.66 billion","10 million","0.1 billion"],"answer":0,"solution":"No solution available. ","difficulty":"Medium","source":"IAT (IISER) 2024","_marksId":"67fdff72378e1624ea7b6b91"},{"id":23821,"subject":"Biology","chapter":"Biotechnology - Principles and Processes","exam":"Engineering","examName":"IAT (IISER)","q":"The map of a 4000 base pair (bp) plasmid DNA marking the locations of different restriction enzyme cut sites is shown in the figure below. The numbers in brackets indicate the base pair positions where the enzymes cut. This plasmid is completely digested first with the combination of restriction enzymes PstI and HindIII, and then with EcoRI an